\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $X$ be a complete Riemannian $n$-manifold with scalar curvature bounded below by a positive constant. Does there exist a constant $C_n$, depending only on dimension, such that its filling radius satisfies
\[\operatorname{FillRad}(X)\leq C_n\bigl(\inf_{x\in X}\operatorname{Sc}(x)\bigr)^{-2}?\]
The filling radius measures the neighborhood size needed to fill the fundamental cycle after the metric-space embedding used in its definition.
The catalogue transcription gives exponent $-2$; the cited original is needed to determine whether this exponent is correct.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://www.ihes.fr/~gromov/wp-content/uploads/2018/08/101-problemsOct1-2017.pdf}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $X$ be a complete Riemannian $n$-manifold with scalar curvature bounded below by a positive constant. Does there exist a constant $C_n$, depending only on dimension, such that its filling radius satisfies
\[\operatorname{FillRad}(X)\leq C_n\bigl(\inf_{x\in X}\operatorname{Sc}(x)\bigr)^{-2}?\]
The filling radius measures the neighborhood size needed to fill the fundamental cycle after the metric-space embedding used in its definition.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 6700034 (AMR-066-0034). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://www.ihes.fr/~gromov/wp-content/uploads/2018/08/101-problemsOct1-2017.pdf}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:6700034}

